#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084446 :tutorial/content "Given a collection of $n$ objects, there are $n!$ ways to arrange those objects in a sequence.\n\n- For example, suppose we have $n=2$ objects: $A$ and $B.$ Then there are $2!=2 \\cdot 1 = 2$ arrangements:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A,B}_{1\\text{st way}}, \\quad \\underbrace{B,A}_{2\\text{nd way}}\n$$\n\n- Likewise, suppose we have $n=3$ objects: $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Then there are $3!=3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6$ arrangements:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A,B,C}_{1\\text{st way}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{A,C,B}_{2\\text{nd way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{B,A,C}_{3\\text{rd way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{B,C,A}_{4\\text{th way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{C,A,B}_{5\\text{th way}},\\quad \n\\underbrace{C,B,A}_{6\\text{th way}}\n$$\n\nThis pattern extends to any number of objects." :tutorial/id #uuid "0753e8b5-a022-5644-84fc-f43f1dcf6761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3680 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084447 :tutorial/content "The mathematical name for an arrangement is a **permutation**. With a permutation, *the order matters*. This means that for the letters $A,B,C,D,E$, the permutation\n\n$$\nA,B,C,D,E\n$$\n\n    is different from\n\n$$\nE,D,C,B,A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "29dee738-c115-51e6-b129-3ddb6d812d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3681 :tutorial/title "Permutations"}]}
